  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 60af0432a3c869e48a591344a8dafd71, type: 3}
  m_Name: ResCoordDisc 6
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  question: "You overhear a research nurse talking to one of the lab researchers
    about using stored patient tissue samples to help study a specific cancer mutation.
    The researcher tells the nurse that you are double-checking that the research
    team has the right approvals and patient permissions to use those samples first
    \u2014 an important aspect of the research coordinator\u2019s role in early discovery.\n\nHow
    does the research coordinator\u2019s role differ from a lab researcher\u2019s
    role in the Discovery stage?"
  explanation: "In early discovery, lab researchers carry out the hands-on scientific
    work \u2014 studying tissue samples, analyzing cancer cells, and identifying
    new mutations. Research coordinators, on the other hand, help ensure that any
    patient samples or data used in those experiments follow proper ethical, legal,
    and privacy standards. While research coordinators aren\u2019t running experiments
    or coming up with scientific questions, they make sure research teams follow
    the rules and keep things organized from the very start."
  correctAnswer: 'B: The research coordinator focuses on approvals and proper use
    of patient samples, while the lab researcher conducts experiments and runs tests
    in the lab'
  incorrectAnswer1: 'A: The research coordinator runs laboratory tests, while the
    lab researcher reviews patient consent forms'
  incorrectAnswer2: 'C: The research coordinator decides which scientific questions
    the research team should study, while the lab researcher gathers patient tissue
    samples'
  incorrectAnswer3: 'D: None of the above'
  questionRole: Research Coordinator
  questionType: MultipleChoice
  questionStage: Discovery
